{"pack": "m1learn", "voice": "girl", "speaker": {"af_heart": 1.0}, "audio": "m1learn-20261106.mp4", "model": "Kokoro-82M v1.0 (Apache 2.0) via kokoro-onnx (MIT); misaki phonemizer (Apache 2.0)", "gap": 0.45, "clips": {"Hmm, not quite.": [0.0, 1.023], "Almost! Let\u2019s look again.": [1.473, 1.983], "Good try!": [3.905, 1.003], "Hmm, not that one.": [5.358, 1.127], "Let me show you!": [6.935, 1.149], "Adding means putting groups together to find how many in all.": [8.534, 3.447], "Here are 8 fish and 5 fish. How many in all?": [12.431, 2.945], "Here is a sailor's trick: make a ten!": [15.825, 2.508], "8 needs 2 more to make 10. So take 2 fish from the group of 5.": [18.783, 4.192], "Now we have 10 and 3 more. 10 + 3 = 13. So 8 + 5 = 13!": [23.425, 6.453], "9 crabs and 4 crabs. How many crabs in all?": [30.329, 3.276], "9 + 1 = 10, and 3 more makes 13. So 9 + 4 = 13.": [34.055, 6.044], "9 needs 1 more to make 10.": [40.549, 1.989], "Take 1 from the 4. Now you have 10 and 3 more.": [42.987, 3.158], "12 is one too few. 9 needs 1 more to make 10. Then add what is left of the 4.": [46.595, 5.856], "14 is one too many. Make 10 first: 9 + 1. Then count what is left of the 4.": [52.901, 6.229], "12, 13, or 14?": [59.58, 1.962], "7 + 3 = 10, and 3 more makes 13. So 7 + 6 = 13.": [61.992, 6.101], "7 needs 3 more to make 10.": [68.543, 2.031], "Take 3 from the 6. Now you have 10 and 3 more.": [71.024, 3.276], "11 is too few. 7 + 3 makes 10. How many are left of the 6?": [74.75, 5.034], "12 is one too few. 7 + 3 makes 10, and 6 has 3 more left.": [80.234, 5.431], "11, 13, or 12?": [86.115, 2.069], "You counted the whole catch: 13 fish! Thank you, {name}!": [88.633, 3.12], "Jonah has 6 fish and catches 8 more. How many fish in all?": [92.203, 3.543], "8 + 2 = 10, and 4 more makes 14.": [96.196, 3.222], "8 needs 2 more to make 10.": [99.868, 1.775], "Take 2 from the 6. Now you have 10 and 4 more.": [102.094, 3.105], "13 is one too few. 8 + 2 makes 10. How many are left of the 6?": [105.649, 5.215], "15 is one too many. Make 10 first: 8 + 2. Then add the rest of the 6.": [111.314, 5.919], "13, 14, or 15?": [117.682, 2.164], "A ruler tells us how long something is.": [120.296, 2.391], "Each space between two numbers is one inch. Always start at the 0!": [123.138, 4.172], "Before I build the real bridge, I make a small model. Here is a model plank.": [127.76, 4.617], "I put one end of the plank on the 0. The other end is at the 5.": [132.827, 3.999], "So the plank is 5 inches long!": [137.276, 2.041], "How long is this plank?": [139.767, 1.592], "The plank starts at 0 and ends at 3. It is 3 inches long.": [141.809, 4.085], "Look where the plank ends.": [146.344, 1.518], "The plank starts at 0. Count each inch to its end.": [148.312, 3.276], "The plank goes past the 2. Look where the plank ends.": [152.038, 3.221], "The plank does not reach the 4. Look where it ends.": [155.709, 2.902], "2 inches, 3 inches, or 4 inches?": [159.061, 2.789], "It starts at 0 and ends at 6, so it is 6 inches long.": [162.3, 3.818], "Find the number right under the end of the plank.": [166.568, 2.616], "The end of the plank is at the 6.": [169.634, 1.986], "The plank does not reach the 7. Look where it ends.": [172.07, 3.157], "The plank goes past the 5. Look where it ends.": [175.676, 2.933], "6 inches, 7 inches, or 5 inches?": [179.06, 3.089], "I know how long each board must be. I cut planks to match my model. Take them to Penny at the broken bridge!": [182.598, 6.569], "It starts at 0 and ends at 4, so it is 4 inches long.": [189.617, 3.681], "Start at the 0.": [193.748, 1.222], "Count the inches to the end of the plank.": [195.42, 2.265], "The plank goes past the 3. Look where it ends.": [198.135, 2.889], "The plank does not reach the 5. Look where it ends.": [201.474, 3.039], "4 inches, 3 inches, or 5 inches?": [204.963, 2.918], "Shapes have straight sides and corners.": [208.331, 2.147], "Triangle: 3 sides. Square, rectangle: 4. Pentagon: 5. Hexagon: 6!": [210.928, 5.871], "Let's count the sides of this shape together.": [217.248, 2.243], "1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6. It has 6 sides.": [219.941, 3.455], "A shape with 6 sides is a hexagon!": [223.847, 2.414], "How many sides does this shape have?": [226.711, 1.996], "It has 3 sides. A shape with 3 sides is a triangle.": [229.157, 3.851], "Touch each side with your finger as you count.": [233.457, 2.243], "Count: 1, 2, \u2026": [236.151, 1.601], "Count again, one side at a time. Touch each side only once.": [238.202, 3.903], "5 is too many. Count each side once with your finger.": [242.555, 3.447], "3, 4, or 5?": [246.452, 1.541], "What is this shape called?": [248.443, 1.487], "It has 5 sides, so it is a pentagon.": [250.379, 2.636], "Count its sides first.": [253.465, 1.485], "It has 5 sides. A shape with 5 sides is a\u2026": [255.401, 3.611], "A hexagon has 6 sides. Count the sides of this shape.": [259.462, 3.498], "A square has 4 sides. Count the sides of this shape.": [263.41, 3.253], "hexagon, pentagon, or square?": [267.113, 2.105], "You know your shapes! Now let's build the bridge. Each piece of the plan needs the right shape.": [269.669, 5.119], "How many sides does a hexagon have?": [275.238, 2.263], "A hexagon has 6 sides.": [277.951, 1.899], "Count the sides of the shape.": [280.301, 1.645], "Hex- shapes have more than 5 sides.": [282.395, 2.147], "A shape with 5 sides is a pentagon. A hexagon has more.": [284.992, 3.98], "8 is too many. Count the sides of the hexagon in the picture.": [289.422, 3.734], "5, 6, or 8?": [293.606, 1.445], "When we read, we can ask: WHO is in it? WHERE does it happen? WHEN? WHY? The answers hide in the words!": [295.501, 6.255], "A sentence is a group of words that tells one idea. It starts with a big letter and ends with a dot.": [302.206, 6.399], "Let's read Keeper Ada's logbook together.": [309.055, 2.488], "Who climbed the stairs? I look in the story\u2026 \"Keeper Ada climbed the tall stairs.\"": [311.993, 4.769], "So the answer is Keeper Ada! I found it in the words.": [317.212, 3.221], "WHEN did the lamp go out?": [320.882, 1.633], "The story starts: \"It was the night of the big storm.\" Then the lamp went out.": [322.966, 4.471], "Read the first words of the story again.": [327.886, 2.242], "The story starts with \"It was the night of\u2026\"": [330.578, 2.839], "The morning came later in the story. Read the first words: when did the lamp go out?": [333.868, 5.121], "There is no afternoon in the story. Read the first words again.": [339.438, 3.701], "On the night of the big storm, In the morning, after the storm, or On a rainy afternoon?": [343.59, 5.119], "WHY did Ada want the light to shine?": [349.159, 1.999], "Ada said, \"The light must shine for the ships.\" Ships need the light to stay safe.": [351.608, 4.711], "Find what Ada said.": [356.769, 1.381], "Ada said, \"The light must shine for the\u2026\"": [358.6, 2.553], "Ada did not need the light to read. Who needs a lighthouse at night?": [361.603, 3.647], "A lighthouse is not for cooking. Who sails at night and needs to see?": [365.7, 4.011], "For the ships, To read a book, or To cook dinner?": [370.162, 2.759], "The storm put the lamp out. Let's help Keeper Ada light it again!": [373.37, 3.819], "Read: \"Mo fixed the boat at the harbor.\" WHERE did Mo fix the boat?": [377.64, 3.616], "The sentence says \"at the harbor.\"": [381.706, 1.946], "WHERE asks about a place.": [384.102, 1.613], "Find the place word in the sentence: \"\u2026at the ___.\"": [386.165, 2.893], "The sentence does not say forest. Find the WHERE words: \"at the \u2026\".": [389.508, 4.645], "The sentence does not say farm. Read it again and find the place.": [394.604, 3.859], "At the harbor, In the forest, or At the farm?": [398.913, 2.655], "Some words name a whole group.": [402.018, 1.805], "A group of fish is called a school. A group of birds is called a flock.": [404.273, 4.107], "Look at all these cows standing together.": [408.83, 2.223], "A group of cows is called a herd.": [411.503, 2.007], "So I write: \"The herd is eating grass.\"": [413.96, 2.463], "This letter got wet in the storm. Which word fits? \"A ___ of fish swam by the pier.\"": [416.873, 4.853], "A group of fish is a school. \"A school of fish swam by the pier.\"": [422.176, 3.97], "Remember: what do we call a group of fish?": [426.596, 2.39], "A fish group has the same name as the place kids learn.": [429.436, 3.245], "A flock is a group of birds. These are fish.": [433.131, 2.922], "A herd is a group of cows. These are fish.": [436.503, 2.753], "school, flock, or herd?": [439.706, 1.593], "Which word fits? \"A ___ of birds flew over the lighthouse.\"": [441.749, 3.252], "A group of birds is a flock. \"A flock of birds flew over the lighthouse.\"": [445.451, 4.222], "Think about a group of birds.": [450.123, 1.729], "Birds fly together in a\u2026": [452.302, 1.934], "A herd is a group of cows. These are birds.": [454.686, 2.922], "A school is a group of fish. These are birds.": [458.057, 2.953], "herd, flock, or school?": [461.461, 1.639], "Perfect! The letters make sense again. Now I can deliver them all over the island. Thank you, {name}!": [463.55, 5.769], "Which word fits? \"The farmer's ___ of cows is in the field.\"": [469.769, 3.34], "A group of cows is a herd.": [473.559, 1.731], "What do we call a group of cows?": [475.74, 1.774], "It is not a school (fish) or a flock (birds).": [477.964, 2.649], "A flock is a group of birds, not cows.": [481.063, 2.549], "A school is a group of fish, not cows.": [484.062, 2.455], "Sometimes we read a word we don't know. Don't worry! The other words in the sentence can give us clues.": [486.967, 6.015], "Read: \"The storm made the sky dark and gloomy.\"": [493.433, 2.701], "Do I know \"gloomy\"? Hmm. The clue word \"dark\" is right next to it.": [496.584, 3.542], "So gloomy means dark and sad-looking!": [500.576, 2.202], "\"The sailor was so weary after the long trip that he fell asleep.\" What does weary mean?": [503.228, 5.045], "He fell asleep after a long trip, so weary means very tired.": [508.723, 3.841], "What did the sailor do at the end?": [513.014, 1.861], "He fell asleep after a long trip. So he was\u2026": [515.325, 3.381], "Being happy does not make you fall asleep. Read the clue again.": [519.156, 3.596], "Being fast does not make you fall asleep. What makes you sleepy?": [523.202, 3.809], "very tired, very happy, or very fast?": [527.461, 2.804], "\"The lighthouse beam swept across the dark sea.\" What is a beam?": [530.715, 3.713], "A lighthouse makes light, so a beam is a line of light.": [534.878, 3.104], "What does a lighthouse make at night?": [538.432, 1.838], "A lighthouse makes light. The beam goes across the dark sea.": [540.72, 3.563], "A lighthouse does not send fish across the sea. What does it make?": [544.733, 3.734], "You cannot see a sound in the dark. The clue is the dark sea.": [548.917, 3.509], "a kind of fish, a line of light, or a loud, deep sound?": [552.877, 3.477], "Great clue hunting! The storm washed the words off my signs. Now I can paint them again, nice and clear.": [556.803, 6.428], "\"The bread was so enormous that it did not fit in the basket.\" What does enormous mean?": [563.681, 4.971], "It did not fit in the basket, so enormous means very big.": [569.103, 3.659], "Find the clue: it did not fit in the basket.": [573.211, 2.667], "Things that do not fit are usually too\u2026": [576.329, 2.647], "A small loaf would fit in the basket. This one did not fit.": [579.425, 3.777], "Cold bread can still fit in a basket. Why did it not fit?": [583.652, 3.66], "very big, very small, or very cold?": [587.762, 2.553], "Our world has land and water. Land: a beach, a hill, an island. Water: an ocean, a river, a lake, a pond.": [590.765, 7.814], "Let's look at a model of our island. A model is a small picture of a real place.": [599.029, 4.959], "See? There is water all around the land. Land with water all around it is an island.": [604.438, 5.399], "The big salty water around it is the ocean!": [610.287, 2.563], "Water that flows across the land in a long line is a\u2026": [613.3, 3.539], "A river is water that flows across the land.": [617.289, 2.765], "This water moves. It flows from one place to another.": [620.504, 3.437], "On our island it runs between the village and the harbor.": [624.391, 3.32], "A pond is small and still. This water moves in a long line.": [628.161, 3.905], "An ocean is huge and salty, all around the land. This water flows across the land.": [632.515, 5.633], "pond, river, or ocean?": [638.598, 1.646], "A small body of still water on land is a\u2026": [640.694, 2.813], "A pond is a small body of still water.": [643.956, 2.468], "It is small, and the water stays still.": [646.874, 2.36], "Frogs and lily pads live in one in our forest.": [649.684, 2.979], "An ocean is huge, not small. This water is small and still.": [653.113, 3.713], "A river flows and moves. This water is still.": [657.276, 3.019], "pond, ocean, or river?": [660.744, 1.881], "You read our island like a scientist: ocean, river, pond and beaches!": [663.076, 4.541], "Land with water all around it is an\u2026": [668.066, 2.391], "An island is land with water all around it.": [670.907, 2.691], "Think about our home!": [674.048, 1.347], "It is land, not water.": [675.846, 1.635], "An ocean is water, not land. Look for land with water all around it.": [677.93, 4.309], "A river is water that flows. The answer is a kind of land.": [682.689, 3.777], "island, ocean, or river?": [686.916, 2.013], "A habitat is a home for plants and animals. Ponds, forests and beaches are habitats. Each has its own life.": [689.379, 7.295], "Let's observe this pond. I look closely and count the different living things.": [697.124, 4.511], "I see lily pads, reeds, a frog, a fish and a dragonfly. That is 5 kinds!": [702.085, 5.267], "Many kinds of living things share one habitat.": [707.802, 2.966], "Look at this forest habitat. Which group lives here?": [711.218, 2.976], "In the forest we see trees, birds and butterflies.": [714.644, 3.36], "Look at the picture. What do you see?": [718.454, 2.159], "A forest has lots of trees\u2026": [721.063, 2.156], "Crabs and sea stars live by the sea. This picture is a forest.": [723.669, 4.084], "Fish and seaweed live in water. This picture is a forest.": [728.203, 3.573], "trees, birds and butterflies, crabs, sea stars and sea snails, or fish and seaweed?": [732.226, 5.667], "Which habitat has MORE kinds of living things?": [738.344, 2.659], "Habitat A has 5 kinds of living things. Habitat B has only 1 kind.": [741.452, 5.269], "Count the different living things in each picture.": [747.171, 2.543], "One picture has 5 kinds. How many does the other have?": [750.164, 3.498], "Habitat B has fewer kinds. Count the living things in each picture.": [754.112, 4.246], "Habitat A, or Habitat B?": [758.808, 2.253], "Go observe! Frogs by the pond, crabs on the beach, robins in the meadow. Every habitat is full of life!": [761.511, 6.827], "A habitat is\u2026": [768.788, 1.511], "A habitat is the home where plants and animals live.": [770.749, 3.455], "A pond and a forest are habitats.": [774.655, 2.219], "Who lives in a pond or a forest?": [777.324, 1.966], "Animals live in a habitat, but a habitat is not an animal.": [779.74, 3.873], "A habitat is full of life. Think of the pond: who lives there?": [784.063, 3.754], "a home for plants and animals, a kind of animal that lives in ponds, or a place where nothing lives?": [788.267, 6.101], "Things are made of materials: wood, stone, rope, glass.": [794.818, 3.813], "Observe and sort them: hard or soft? Stiff or bendy?": [799.081, 3.687], "Let me test this rope. I bend it\u2026 it bends easily!": [803.218, 3.446], "I can even tie it in a knot. Something that bends easily is flexible.": [807.114, 4.159], "So rope is flexible!": [811.723, 1.549], "Which material is hard?": [813.723, 1.541], "Stone is hard. Wool and rope are soft and bendy.": [815.713, 3.213], "Hard things do not squish when you press them.": [819.377, 2.415], "Think of a rock on the beach.": [822.242, 1.676], "Wool is soft and squishy. Which one does not squish?": [824.368, 3.115], "Rope bends and is soft. Which one is hard?": [827.933, 2.69], "wool, stone, or rope?": [831.073, 1.597], "We need to tie the bridge pieces together. Which material is flexible enough to tie?": [833.12, 4.788], "Rope is flexible, so we can tie knots with it.": [838.358, 2.859], "To tie something, it must bend.": [841.667, 2.017], "Which one can make a knot?": [844.135, 1.645], "Glass is stiff and can break. It cannot bend into a knot.": [846.229, 3.615], "Stone is hard. It cannot bend into a knot.": [850.294, 2.827], "glass, stone, or rope?": [853.571, 1.651], "Here is strong, flexible rope for the bridge! Take it to Penny. Hard wood and bendy rope make a great team.": [855.671, 6.591], "Which one is flexible (bendy)?": [862.712, 1.925], "A rope bends easily, so it is flexible.": [865.087, 2.657], "Flexible means it bends easily.": [868.194, 2.094], "Which one could you tie in a knot?": [870.738, 1.891], "A rock is hard. It does not bend.": [873.079, 2.323], "A glass jar is stiff. It breaks if you bend it.": [875.852, 2.935], "a rope, a rock, or a glass jar?": [879.237, 2.225], "A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. It is how a bird sees it, flying high in the sky.": [881.912, 5.909], "On a map, small pictures called symbols show where things are.": [888.271, 3.34], "This tree symbol shows a forest. This blue line shows a river.": [892.061, 3.895], "The key tells us what each symbol means.": [896.406, 2.211], "On this map, what does the blue line show?": [899.066, 2.434], "The key says the blue line is a river.": [901.95, 2.148], "Look at the key next to the map.": [904.548, 1.763], "Blue is the color of water.": [906.761, 1.721], "Roads are not blue on this map. Look at the key: what does blue show?": [908.932, 4.021], "A fence is not blue on this map. Look at the key to see what blue means.": [913.404, 4.32], "a road, a river, or a fence?": [918.174, 2.091], "Look at the Atlas page. What is right next to the lighthouse?": [920.715, 3.327], "The lighthouse stands on a cliff right next to the sea.": [924.492, 2.915], "Find the lighthouse symbol on the map.": [927.857, 1.976], "What color is all around the lighthouse's cliff?": [930.283, 2.465], "The farm is far up the island, not next to the lighthouse.": [933.198, 3.242], "The forest is far away on the other side. Look right next to the lighthouse.": [936.89, 4.468], "the sea, the farm, or the forest?": [941.808, 2.177], "Now you can read the Atlas page! But first: explorer training!": [944.435, 4.171], "A map shows a place as if we look at it from\u2026": [949.056, 2.929], "A map shows a place seen from above.": [952.434, 2.093], "Think of a bird flying high.": [954.977, 1.699], "The bird looks down from\u2026": [957.126, 1.751], "From the side we see a picture, like a photo. A map shows a place the way a flying bird sees it.": [959.326, 5.835], "From the front we see the front of a house. A map shows a place the way a flying bird sees it.": [965.611, 5.589], "above, the side, or the front?": [971.649, 1.941], "We can make our own map! We draw the things in a place. We put each one where it really is.": [974.04, 5.023], "The storm washed my board map. Let's draw it again.": [979.513, 3.148], "The workshop is right next to the bakery in the village.": [983.111, 2.969], "So on the map, I draw the workshop next to the bakery!": [986.529, 3.138], "The farm is at the top of the island, far from the sea. Where does it go on our map?": [990.117, 4.704], "Spot A is at the top of the map, where the farm really is.": [995.271, 3.401], "The farm is at the TOP of the island.": [999.123, 2.051], "Find the spot at the top of the map.": [1001.624, 1.933], "Spot B is at the bottom, by the sea. The farm is at the top.": [1004.007, 3.818], "Spot C is not at the top. Find the spot at the top, far from the sea.": [1008.275, 4.329], "spot A, spot B, or spot C?": [1013.054, 2.187], "The beach is at the bottom of the island, by the sea. Where does the beach go?": [1015.691, 4.083], "Spot B is at the bottom, next to the sea. That is where the beach really is.": [1020.223, 4.394], "The beach is at the BOTTOM, by the water.": [1025.067, 2.179], "Find the spot next to the blue sea at the bottom.": [1027.697, 2.787], "Spot A is at the top. The beach is at the bottom, by the sea.": [1030.933, 3.649], "Spot C is not at the bottom by the sea. Look at the bottom of the map.": [1035.032, 4.043], "Our new map is ready! Now nobody gets lost. Great mapmaking, {name}!": [1039.525, 4.144], "When we make a map, we put each place\u2026": [1044.119, 2.686], "On a map, each place goes where it really is. So the map helps us find our way.": [1047.254, 4.673], "A map must help people find places.": [1052.377, 2.347], "The bakery is next to the workshop. On the map, it is\u2026": [1055.174, 3.835], "If we put places anywhere, the map would not help anyone find them.": [1059.46, 3.936], "A map is not a list of names. Each place goes in its real spot.": [1063.846, 4.0], "where it really is, anywhere we like, or in ABC order by name?": [1068.296, 3.957], "Natural things come from nature: rivers, trees, beaches. People make other things: houses, roads, bridges.": [1072.703, 7.419], "Let's look through my telescope at our island.": [1080.572, 2.532], "I see the forest. People did not build it, so it is natural!": [1083.554, 3.807], "I see the pier. People built it with wood, so it is made by people.": [1087.81, 4.148], "Is the river natural or made by people?": [1092.408, 2.531], "The river comes from nature, so it is natural.": [1095.389, 2.805], "Did people build the river?": [1098.644, 1.677], "Rivers come from rain and springs in nature.": [1100.772, 2.657], "People did not make the river. It comes from nature.": [1103.879, 3.043], "natural, or made by people?": [1107.372, 1.901], "Which one was made by people?": [1109.722, 1.795], "People built the lighthouse, so it is made by people.": [1111.968, 3.051], "Which one was built with bricks or wood?": [1115.469, 2.105], "Nature did not build towers.": [1118.024, 1.931], "People did not make the ocean. It comes from nature.": [1120.405, 3.137], "Trees grow on their own. Which one did people build?": [1123.992, 3.127], "the ocean, the lighthouse, or the forest?": [1127.568, 2.403], "A good map shows natural places and places people made. Great eyes, {name}!": [1130.421, 4.468], "Which one is natural?": [1135.339, 1.528], "A beach is natural. Roads and houses are made by people.": [1137.317, 3.605], "Natural things come from nature.": [1141.371, 2.02], "People did not build it with tools.": [1143.841, 2.009], "People build roads. Look for the one that comes from nature.": [1146.3, 3.33], "People build houses. Look for the one that comes from nature.": [1150.08, 3.404], "a beach, a road, or a house?": [1153.934, 1.905], "Real history! Before electricity, lighthouses burned oil lamps. Keepers carried oil up and trimmed the wicks.": [1156.289, 6.911], "To compare is to look at two things. How are they the same? How are they different?": [1163.651, 4.595], "Now let's compare. Long ago: an oil flame, and a keeper climbing the stairs every day.": [1168.695, 5.587], "Today: real lighthouses use electric lights. Workers still check them, but nobody carries oil up anymore.": [1174.733, 6.583], "Long ago and today: same job, different ways!": [1181.765, 3.207], "Which light did lighthouses use LONG AGO?": [1185.422, 2.361], "Long ago, before electricity, lighthouses burned oil in lamps.": [1188.233, 4.009], "Long ago there was no electricity.": [1192.692, 2.179], "Keepers carried oil up the stairs for it.": [1195.321, 2.276], "Long ago there was no electricity, so there were no light bulbs.": [1198.047, 3.541], "an oil lamp, or an electric light bulb?": [1202.038, 2.487], "Long ago, what did keepers carry up the lighthouse stairs?": [1204.975, 3.273], "Keepers carried fuel, like oil, up the stairs for the lamp.": [1208.698, 3.729], "What did the flame need to keep burning?": [1212.877, 2.115], "Long ago there was no electricity: no light bulbs, no batteries.": [1215.442, 4.079], "Long ago there were no light bulbs. The flame needed something to burn.": [1219.971, 4.085], "Long ago lights did not use batteries. The flame needed something to burn.": [1224.506, 4.427], "new light bulbs, fuel for the lamp, or batteries for the light?": [1229.383, 3.625], "Same job, different ways! In our story, my lighthouse guides the Atlas pages home.": [1233.457, 5.323], "Today, most real lighthouse lights are\u2026": [1239.231, 2.503], "Today real lighthouses use electric lights.": [1242.184, 2.899], "Long ago they used oil. What do we use today?": [1245.533, 2.765], "The same thing that lights your house at night.": [1248.749, 2.212], "Candles are too weak to be seen far out at sea. Think of lights in houses today.": [1251.411, 4.737], "A campfire on top of a tall tower? The wind would blow it out. Think of today.": [1256.597, 4.629], "electric, candles, or campfires?": [1261.676, 2.377], "Things happen in order: first, next, then, last. Order helps us understand what happened in the past.": [1264.503, 6.513], "Here is a little flower's story.": [1271.466, 1.872], "First, a seed. Next, a small sprout. Last, a big flower.": [1273.788, 4.2], "See? First, next, last!": [1278.438, 1.981], "Put the storm night in order. Tap what happened FIRST, then the next one\u2026": [1280.869, 4.445], "First the wind blew, next the lamp went out, last the pages flew away.": [1285.764, 4.266], "What started the trouble?": [1290.48, 1.454], "First the wind blew. What did the wind do to the lamp?": [1292.384, 3.061], "Put my life in order, from first to last.": [1295.896, 2.585], "First a baby, next a kid, then a grown-up keeper. Last, Grandpa Tom today.": [1298.93, 5.115], "Think about a life in order: which comes next?": [1304.496, 2.7], "After a baby comes a kid. What comes after a kid?": [1307.646, 3.255], "First the storm came, then the light went out. Clever explorer!": [1311.35, 3.744], "A seed, a sprout and a flower. Which comes FIRST?": [1315.544, 3.005], "First the seed, next the sprout, last the flower.": [1318.999, 3.046], "What do we put in the ground?": [1322.495, 1.539], "A plant starts small.": [1324.484, 1.464], "The flower comes after the plant grows. What do you plant first?": [1326.398, 3.669], "A sprout grows out of something. What do you plant first?": [1330.517, 3.147], "the flower, the seed, or the sprout?": [1334.114, 2.043], "We learn about the past from historical sources.": [1336.606, 2.734], "Old photos, letters, diaries and maps are sources. Old objects are, too.": [1339.79, 5.143], "Let's open my grandma's memory box.": [1345.383, 2.518], "This old photo shows our bakery long, long ago. Look at the old oven!": [1348.351, 4.269], "The photo is a historical source. It tells us how the bakery looked back then.": [1353.069, 4.788], "Which one is a historical source?": [1358.307, 2.155], "An old letter was written long ago. So it can tell us about the past.": [1360.913, 4.01], "A source tells us about the past.": [1365.373, 1.987], "Which one was written long ago?": [1367.81, 1.721], "A new toy is from today. It cannot tell us about long ago.": [1369.981, 3.787], "You drew it today, so it is new. It is not from long ago.": [1374.218, 3.467], "an old letter, a brand-new toy, or a picture you drew today?": [1378.135, 3.553], "Grandma's old recipe card can tell us\u2026": [1382.138, 2.895], "It is an old recipe, so it tells us how people baked long ago.": [1385.483, 4.033], "The card is old. It is about the past.": [1389.965, 2.549], "A recipe tells how to bake something.": [1392.965, 2.105], "An old card cannot tell the future. It tells about the past.": [1395.52, 3.764], "Tomorrow has not happened yet. The card is about the past.": [1399.734, 3.583], "how people baked long ago, what the weather will be tomorrow, or what Grandma will cook tomorrow?": [1403.767, 5.418], "My memory box is full of sources, and now you can read them. History lives in old things! A muffin, {name}?": [1409.635, 6.259], "Which one can teach us about the past?": [1416.344, 2.252], "An old diary was written long ago, so it is a historical source.": [1419.046, 4.084], "It must be from long ago.": [1423.58, 1.646], "Someone wrote in it a long time ago.": [1425.676, 2.105], "An empty notebook has nothing in it. Nobody wrote in it long ago.": [1428.231, 3.999], "A toy from today is new. It cannot teach us about long ago.": [1432.68, 3.647], "an old diary, a new empty notebook, or a toy you got today?": [1436.777, 3.585], "Subtracting means taking away.": [1440.812, 1.923], "Here are 12 planks. The storm broke 5 of them. How many good planks are left?": [1443.185, 4.67], "A carpenter\u2019s trick: take away to get to 10 first!": [1448.305, 3.114], "12 \u2212 2 = 10. That takes away 2 of the 5.": [1451.869, 3.903], "Now take away the other 3: 10 \u2212 3 = 7. So 12 \u2212 5 = 7!": [1456.222, 5.909], "9 nails. Bramble uses 4. How many nails are left?": [1462.581, 3.868], "Count back 4 from 9: 8, 7, 6, 5. So 9 \u2212 4 = 5.": [1466.899, 5.739], "Start at 9 and count back 4.": [1473.088, 2.02], "9\u2026 8, 7\u2026 keep going until you count 4 numbers back.": [1475.558, 3.703], "6 is one too many. Start at 9 and count back 4.": [1479.71, 3.511], "4 is the nails he used. How many are LEFT after he uses them?": [1483.671, 4.138], "6, 5, or 4?": [1488.259, 1.542], "14 \u2212 4 = 10, and 10 \u2212 2 = 8. So 14 \u2212 6 = 8.": [1490.251, 6.293], "Take away 4 first to get to 10.": [1496.994, 2.094], "14 \u2212 4 = 10. Then take away 2 more.": [1499.538, 3.606], "9 is one too many. Take 4 to get to 10, then 2 more.": [1503.594, 3.915], "7 is one too few. Take 4 to get to 10, then take 2 more.": [1507.958, 4.3], "8, 9, or 7?": [1512.708, 1.541], "The roof needs 13 planks. Bramble has 8. How many more does he need?": [1514.699, 4.647], "8 and 5 more make 13, so 13 \u2212 8 = 5. He needs 5 more planks.": [1519.796, 6.207], "How far is it from 8 to 13?": [1526.453, 2.115], "8 + 2 = 10, and 3 more is 13.": [1529.019, 3.157], "4 is one too few. Count up from 8 to 13.": [1532.625, 3.287], "6 is one too many. Count up from 8 to 13.": [1536.363, 3.297], "4, 6, or 5?": [1540.11, 1.587], "7 good planks are left, so we cut 5 new ones for the bridge!": [1542.147, 3.979], "15 \u2212 5 = 10, and 10 \u2212 2 = 8.": [1546.575, 3.883], "Take away 5 first to get to 10.": [1550.909, 2.148], "15 \u2212 5 = 10. Then take away 2 more.": [1553.507, 3.617], "9 is one too many. Take 5 to get to 10, then 2 more.": [1557.574, 4.02], "7 is one too few. Take 5 to get to 10, then take 2 more.": [1562.044, 4.374], "9, 8, or 7?": [1566.868, 1.796], "A story problem asks a math question. Add or take away?": [1569.114, 3.831], "\"How many more?\" means compare: find the difference.": [1573.395, 2.943], "\"I baked 6 pies. Then I baked 3 more. How many pies now?\" More came: add. 6 + 3 = 9 pies.": [1576.788, 7.515], "\"Finn has 3 fish. I have 5. How many more do I have?\" Compare: 5 \u2212 3 = 2. I have 2 more.": [1584.753, 7.269], "Always say the answer with its word: 9 pies, 2 fish!": [1592.472, 3.739], "Maple had 11 muffins. She sold 4. How many muffins are left?": [1596.66, 4.491], "Sold means taking away: 11 \u2212 4 = 7. 7 muffins are left.": [1601.601, 5.108], "Did muffins come or go away?": [1607.159, 1.838], "Sold means they went away: 11 \u2212 4.": [1609.447, 2.977], "She SOLD muffins, so there are fewer, not more. Take away.": [1612.874, 3.401], "6 is one too few. Count back 4 from 11.": [1616.726, 3.286], "7 muffins, 15 muffins, or 6 muffins?": [1620.462, 3.291], "8 kids came for bread. Then 7 more kids came. How many kids came in all?": [1624.202, 4.981], "More came, so add: 8 + 7 = 15. 15 kids came.": [1629.634, 4.906], "More kids came: add.": [1634.99, 1.837], "8 needs 2 more to make 10. 10 and 5 more.": [1637.276, 3.053], "14 is one too few. 8 + 2 makes 10, and 5 more are left.": [1640.779, 4.961], "More kids CAME, so there are more. Add them together.": [1646.19, 2.977], "14 kids, 1 kid, or 15 kids?": [1649.618, 2.763], "Finn ate 5 cookies. Juniper ate 9 cookies. How many MORE did Juniper eat?": [1652.83, 4.94], "Compare: 9 \u2212 5 = 4. Juniper ate 4 more cookies.": [1658.22, 4.559], "\"How many more\" means compare: find the difference.": [1663.23, 2.773], "From 5 to 9: count up 6, 7, 8, 9.": [1666.452, 3.259], "14 is how many they ate together. The question asks how many MORE.": [1670.162, 4.108], "3 is one too few. Count up from 5 to 9.": [1674.72, 3.201], "14 cookies, 4 cookies, or 3 cookies?": [1678.37, 2.955], "Oh no! I had 12 pies and only 5 are left. 7 are missing\u2026 and there are cookie crumbs!": [1681.775, 5.919], "Nell saw 6 boats. Then 6 more came. How many boats now?": [1688.144, 4.026], "More came, so add: 6 + 6 = 12 boats.": [1692.62, 3.69], "More came: add.": [1696.76, 1.467], "6 + 6 is a double.": [1698.676, 1.817], "10 is too few. 6 and 6 more is a double.": [1700.943, 3.137], "More boats CAME, so there are more, not zero.": [1704.53, 2.623], "10 boats, 12 boats, or 0 boats?": [1707.603, 2.659], "Stories have three parts. The beginning tells who and where. The middle has a problem. The end solves it.": [1710.712, 6.069], "A character is a person or an animal in a story. A cat or a crab can be a character too!": [1717.232, 5.963], "Here\u2019s a story: \"Benny the mail dog lived at the post office.\" That\u2019s the beginning: who and where.": [1723.644, 5.654], "\"One windy day, a letter flew out of his bag!\" That\u2019s the middle: the problem.": [1729.748, 4.582], "\"He found it in the sand and brought it home.\" That\u2019s the end: the problem is solved.": [1734.78, 4.747], "\"Kit the cat lived on a boat.\" Which part of the story is this?": [1739.977, 3.562], "It tells who (Kit) and where (a boat): the beginning.": [1743.989, 2.774], "It tells who and where.": [1747.213, 1.454], "Stories start by meeting the characters.": [1749.117, 2.253], "The middle has the problem. This line just tells us who Kit is.": [1751.82, 3.7], "The end tells how things turn out. This line starts the story.": [1755.97, 3.819], "the middle, the beginning, or the end?": [1760.239, 2.237], "What is the problem in the middle of the story?": [1762.926, 2.394], "Her hat was gone: that\u2019s the problem in the middle.": [1765.77, 2.797], "What goes wrong?": [1769.017, 1.193], "\"One morning\u2026\"": [1770.66, 1.439], "That is the beginning, not a problem. What went wrong?": [1772.549, 3.148], "That is how it ends. What went wrong in the middle?": [1776.147, 2.711], "Kit\u2019s red hat was gone, Kit lived on a boat, or Kit put her hat back on?": [1779.309, 4.33], "How does the story end?": [1784.089, 1.529], "Kit finds her hat with the sleepy crab. She puts it back on, and the problem is solved.": [1786.068, 5.121], "How is the problem solved?": [1791.639, 1.721], "Look at the last sentence.": [1793.81, 1.561], "The story does not end that way. What did Kit want back?": [1795.821, 3.531], "That is not in the story. Think about what Kit was looking for.": [1799.801, 3.606], "Kit falls asleep, Kit gets her hat back, or The crab takes more hats?": [1803.857, 4.195], "Our island's story has a problem: the storm! You'll help write the end.": [1808.503, 4.193], "Which part of a story has the problem?": [1813.146, 2.031], "The problem is in the middle.": [1815.627, 1.731], "Think of Benny\u2019s letter.": [1817.809, 1.517], "Between the beginning and the end.": [1819.775, 1.879], "The end is where the problem gets solved. Where does it start?": [1822.105, 3.405], "The beginning tells who and where. The problem comes after that.": [1825.96, 3.647], "the end, the beginning, or the middle?": [1830.057, 2.115], "When big things happen, how does a character respond? How do they feel? What do they do? Look for word clues!": [1832.622, 6.279], "A feeling is how someone feels right now: happy, sad, scared.": [1839.351, 4.045], "A different story: the rain ruined Ben\u2019s sandcastle.": [1843.846, 3.306], "\"Ben felt upset.\" That\u2019s how he felt.": [1847.602, 2.443], "\"He built a new one, higher up the beach.\" That\u2019s what he did. He didn\u2019t give up!": [1850.494, 4.558], "How did Lulu feel when the sign fell?": [1855.502, 2.171], "The story says Lulu felt sad.": [1858.123, 1.956], "Find the feeling word in the story.": [1860.529, 1.98], "\"Lulu felt\u2026\"": [1862.959, 1.405], "Losing her sign was not happy news. Find the feeling word in the story.": [1864.814, 4.459], "Lulu was not tired. Find the feeling word in the story.": [1869.723, 3.522], "happy, sad, or sleepy?": [1873.695, 1.7], "What did Lulu do next?": [1875.845, 1.529], "She painted a new sign, even brighter than before.": [1877.824, 2.979], "Find what she did after she felt sad.": [1881.253, 2.393], "\"Then she got her brushes\u2026\"": [1884.096, 1.836], "Lulu did not go to sleep. She did something to fix the problem.": [1886.382, 3.765], "Lulu did not move away. She did something to fix the problem.": [1890.597, 3.659], "She painted a new sign, She went home to sleep, or She moved away?": [1894.706, 3.807], "How did her friends respond to the new sign?": [1898.962, 2.468], "Her friends cheered: they were happy for her.": [1901.88, 2.508], "Look at the end of the story.": [1904.838, 1.677], "\"Her friends\u2026\"": [1906.966, 1.379], "Crying is for sad news. The new sign was good news.": [1908.795, 3.38], "Her friends did not run away. They liked the new sign.": [1912.625, 3.125], "They cried, They ran away, or They cheered?": [1916.2, 2.326], "After the storm, our village didn't give up either. Everyone is fixing and helping!": [1918.976, 4.865], "A boy loses his kite but keeps looking until he finds it. How does he respond?": [1924.291, 4.607], "He keeps looking: he doesn\u2019t give up.": [1929.348, 2.167], "What does he do?": [1931.965, 1.171], "He keeps looking.": [1933.585, 1.213], "Nothing says he is sleepy. He keeps looking.": [1935.249, 2.74], "He does not forget. He keeps looking until he finds it.": [1938.439, 3.148], "He is sleepy, He doesn\u2019t give up, or He forgets about it?": [1942.037, 3.313], "Water is in oceans, rivers, lakes, ponds, even underground. Most is in oceans.": [1945.8, 5.784], "Rain is liquid: it flows. Ice is solid: it keeps its shape.": [1952.034, 4.083], "On my farm, water is everywhere! My pond, the river, and the well.": [1956.567, 4.292], "The well takes water from under the ground.": [1961.309, 2.223], "In winter, my pond freezes: the water turns solid. Ice!": [1963.982, 3.848], "Where is the biggest amount of water on Earth?": [1968.28, 2.571], "Most of Earth\u2019s water is in the oceans.": [1971.301, 2.201], "Think of the biggest water you know.": [1973.951, 1.881], "Oceans cover huge parts of Earth.": [1976.282, 2.229], "A puddle is tiny. Think of the biggest water on Earth.": [1978.961, 3.347], "Rivers have a lot of water. But the big salty water all over Earth has much, much more.": [1982.759, 5.381], "a puddle, the oceans, or the rivers?": [1988.59, 2.429], "Ice and snow are water that is\u2026": [1991.469, 2.344], "Ice and snow are solid water.": [1994.263, 1.956], "Can you hold ice in your hand?": [1996.669, 1.7], "Water that keeps its shape, like ice, is\u2026": [1998.819, 2.908], "Liquid water flows, like rain. Ice and snow keep their shape.": [2002.177, 3.915], "Steam floats in the air. Ice and snow are hard and keep their shape.": [2006.541, 3.956], "liquid, a gas, like steam, or solid?": [2010.947, 2.478], "Rosa\u2019s well gets water from\u2026": [2013.875, 2.186], "A well brings up water from under the ground.": [2016.511, 2.521], "Wells are deep holes.": [2019.482, 1.528], "There is water down in the ground.": [2021.46, 1.891], "Rain helps, but a well is a deep hole. Where does the well go?": [2023.8, 3.635], "Sea water is salty, and a well is dug deep down into the land. Where does its water hide?": [2027.885, 5.516], "under the ground, the clouds only, or the sea?": [2033.851, 2.573], "Now you know where water hides! My garden can grow again.": [2036.874, 3.393], "Which is solid water?": [2040.717, 1.444], "Snow is solid water.": [2042.611, 1.541], "Solid keeps its shape.": [2044.602, 1.474], "Which one is frozen?": [2046.526, 1.455], "Rain is liquid: it falls and flows. Solid water keeps its shape.": [2048.431, 4.341], "A river is liquid: it flows. Solid water keeps its shape.": [2053.222, 3.956], "rain, snow, or a river?": [2057.628, 1.911], "Earth changes. Quick changes: an earthquake, a volcano. Slow changes: waves wearing away rock over many years.": [2059.989, 7.92], "A source is where we find out about the past. A book or Grandpa\u2019s story can be a source!": [2068.359, 5.269], "Look at the rocks by the harbor. They have smooth holes.": [2074.078, 3.019], "Grandpa Tom says the holes were tiny when he was small. Now they\u2019re bigger. My book says waves did it slowly.": [2077.546, 6.496], "Two sources agree: waves wear away rock SLOWLY.": [2084.492, 3.181], "A volcano explodes. Is that a quick or a slow change?": [2088.123, 3.478], "A volcanic explosion is a quick change.": [2092.051, 2.475], "Does it take minutes or many years?": [2094.976, 2.007], "Volcanic explosions happen fast.": [2097.434, 2.283], "An explosion happens in a moment. That is fast.": [2100.167, 3.04], "slow, or quick?": [2103.657, 1.226], "Waves slowly wear smooth holes in the rocks. Quick or slow?": [2105.333, 3.647], "Waves wearing away rock is a slow change. It takes a very long time.": [2109.43, 4.299], "Does it happen in one day?": [2114.179, 1.635], "Water wears rock little by little.": [2116.264, 2.063], "The word \"slowly\" is in the question. Wearing away rock takes a long time.": [2118.776, 4.489], "How did Mo know the holes were made slowly?": [2123.716, 2.213], "Mo used two sources, Grandpa Tom and a book, as evidence.": [2126.379, 3.987], "Where did Mo get his information?": [2130.816, 1.871], "Grandpa Tom\u2026 and a book.": [2133.137, 2.276], "Mo did not guess. He checked with two sources.": [2135.863, 2.965], "One day is too short to see a slow change! Mo checked two sources.": [2139.278, 4.063], "He guessed, Two sources: Grandpa Tom and a book, or He watched the waves for one whole day?": [2143.79, 5.462], "One stormy night moved sand and rocks fast. Waves shape rocks slowly, too!": [2149.702, 4.903], "Which is a SLOW change?": [2155.056, 1.538], "Waves wear away rock slowly, over many years.": [2157.044, 3.007], "Which one takes many years?": [2160.501, 1.666], "Little by little\u2026": [2162.617, 1.589], "An earthquake lasts only a few seconds. That is fast.": [2164.655, 3.337], "A volcano explodes in a moment. That is fast.": [2168.443, 3.145], "the ground shaking in an earthquake, a volcano exploding, or waves wearing away rock?": [2172.038, 5.397], "A timeline puts events in order, from long ago to now. The earliest event goes first, on the left.": [2177.885, 6.23], "Let\u2019s put our island\u2019s story in order.": [2184.565, 2.148], "First, long ago, people built the lighthouse. Next, Keeper Rosalind chased the Crumb pirates away.": [2187.163, 6.33], "Last, not long ago, the big storm. Long ago \u2192 later \u2192 recently!": [2193.943, 4.667], "Put these in order, from long ago to recently.": [2199.06, 2.882], "First the lighthouse was built. Then Rosalind chased the pirates away. Not long ago came the big storm.": [2202.392, 6.345], "What happened longest ago?": [2209.187, 1.677], "The lighthouse came first; the storm was not long ago.": [2211.315, 3.233], "On a timeline, where does the oldest event go?": [2214.998, 2.795], "The oldest event goes at the beginning of the timeline.": [2218.243, 3.212], "Timelines start long ago.": [2221.905, 1.646], "Oldest first.": [2224.001, 1.213], "The end of a timeline is for the newest things.": [2225.665, 2.7], "The middle is for things in between. The oldest goes first.": [2228.815, 3.531], "at the end (right), at the beginning (left), or in the middle?": [2232.795, 3.255], "Which happened most recently?": [2236.5, 1.754], "The big storm was not long ago: the most recent.": [2238.704, 3.019], "Recently means not long ago.": [2242.173, 1.935], "Which one is closest to now?": [2244.557, 1.709], "The lighthouse was built long ago. What happened just now?": [2246.716, 3.276], "That was long, long ago. What happened just now?": [2250.442, 2.838], "people built the lighthouse, Rosalind chased the pirates, or the big storm?": [2253.73, 4.198], "Now let's add tonight to our timeline: the night we light the lighthouse again!": [2258.378, 4.256], "Which comes first on a timeline?": [2263.084, 1.913], "The event from longest ago comes first.": [2265.447, 2.35], "Timelines go from long ago to now.": [2268.247, 2.138], "This morning is very new, so it goes near the end.": [2270.835, 3.063], "Tomorrow has not happened yet. A timeline starts long ago.": [2274.348, 3.787], "what happened this morning, what happened longest ago, or what happens tomorrow?": [2278.585, 4.236]}}